<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pope Leo X</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1475">1475</a>–<a href="/years/1521">1521</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Leo X, Giovanni di Lorenzo de' Medici, Leone X · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-medici">House of Medici</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pope Leo X, born Giovanni di <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a>, was head of the Catholic Church and leader of the Papal States from 1513 until his death in 1521. The second son of Lorenzo de' Medici of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, he was named a cardinal in 1489 at the age of 13. As pope he oversaw the closing sessions of the Fifth Council of the Lateran and in 1517 waged a costly war to secure his nephew as Duke of Urbino. His granting of indulgences for the rebuilding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-peters-basilica">St. Peter's Basilica</a> was challenged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>, whom his bull Exsurge Domine condemned in 1520. A great patron of the arts, he saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> decorate the Vatican rooms.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pope-leo-x.jpg" alt="Pope Leo X" width="330" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Bini · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raffaello,_ritratto_di_papa_leone_X_tra_i_cardinali_luigi_de'_rossi_e_giulio_de'_medici,_1518,_03.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Giovanni de' Medici was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> on 11 December 1475, the second son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de' Medici</a> and Clarice Orsini. He was destined for a Church career and received the tonsure at seven; by ten he held dozens of benefices. He was educated at his father's humanist court by men such as Angelo Poliziano, Pico della <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a>, and studied theology and canon law at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>. He was admitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals">College of Cardinals</a> in 1492, and took part in the conclave of 1492 that elected Alexander VI.</p><p>He was elected pope on 9 March 1513, when French cardinals were absent, with the support of the younger and noble members of the college. He was not yet a priest, and was ordained on 15 March.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Leo oversaw the closing sessions of the Fifth Council of the Lateran, but did not carry out its reforms. He tried to carve out a state for his brother Giuliano in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, and after Giuliano's death in 1516 turned to his nephew Lorenzo, declaring the Duchy of Urbino forfeit and giving it to him. Francesco Maria della Rovere's attempt to retake it led to an eight-month war in 1517. After a plot by several cardinals to poison him, Leo executed Alfonso Petrucci and on 3 July 1517 named 31 new cardinals, almost without precedent.</p><p>In 1517 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> wrote his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninety-five-theses">Ninety-five Theses</a> on indulgences. Leo at first did not grasp the importance of the movement. Luther met Cardinal Cajetan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in October 1518 but would not retract, and Leo's bull of 1520, Exsurge Domine, condemned him. He remained a patron of the arts and learning: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raphael">Raphael</a> decorated the Vatican rooms, work on St Peter's went forward, and he reorganised the Roman university in 1513.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Leo died suddenly of pneumonia on 1 December 1521, aged 45, and was buried in Santa Maria sopra <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minerva">Minerva</a>. He spent lavishly on the arts, charity, benefices, dynastic wars and his own luxury: within two years he had spent the treasure left by Julius II, and the papal treasury was 400,000 ducats in debt by 1521. Many link his name with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>, because his pontificate did not enforce the Lateran reforms, and the sale of indulgences to fund St Peter's precipitated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. His court included musicians from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He also permitted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew">Hebrew</a> printing press in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pope Leo X” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1519 <b>Pope Leo X</b> called Croatia the forefront of Christianity (Antemurale Christianitatis) for its soldiers' part in the wars with the Ottomans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-croatia-1102-1526">Kingdom of Croatia (1102-1526) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Medici it raised gave the Church two popes, <b>Leo X</b> and Clement VII, and later two queens of France.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-florence">Republic of Florence · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The worldly excesses of the Renaissance Church, as in the era of Alexander VI (1492–1503), intensified under <b>Pope Leo X</b> (1513–1521), whose use of indulgences to raise funds for Saint Peter’s Basilica was a key impetus for Martin Luther’s 95 Theses.</blockquote><a href="/movement/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>To raise money for the rebuilding of St Peter’s Basilica in Rome, <b>Pope Leo X</b> authorised a campaign of indulgence preaching in the lands of Albert of Brandenburg, archbishop of Mainz and Magdeburg, under an agreement that half the proceeds would repay the loan Albert had taken from…</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Outside Wittenberg's Elster Gate, Martin Luther burned <b>Leo X</b>'s bull threatening him with excommunication, along with books of canon law.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-10">10 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Pope Leo X</b> granted Henry VIII the title Fidei Defensor for his treatise attacking Martin Luther.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-11">11 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1504" aria-label="Florence on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4820 613 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Pope Leo X"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5270 883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5270 883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.77,11.25&amp;year=1504">What was here in 1504? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
